From f0154e0000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH 0154e v2] evergo: dump the interrupted stack BEFORE the panic call
Two lessons learned the hard way, both only visible by disassembling the
artifact:

1. #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) compiles to nothing in sleh.c:
   the macro lives in pexpert/pexpert/arm64/board_config.h, which osfmk
   files do not include. v1 produced a binary identical in size to the
   previous build, with zero SWEET STACK strings.

2. panic_plain is a macro for panic() (kern/debug.h), which never returns.
   Placing the dump after the register dump makes it dead code the compiler
   strips - v2 still had no strings while __TEXT_EXEC grew a page from
   unrelated code movement.

This version inserts the dump BEFORE the register dump and uses printf(),
which returns, so the loop survives. Output reaches console + SWEETLOG via
_cnputs (the 0043 CRASH lines already prove that path). A local extern
declaration is provided because sleh.c never sees kern/misc_protos.h.

The dump is an instrument: nothing in the fault path changes, and the 48
extra lines only appear in a panic dump.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
index 24f1abc..3c59f0d 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
@@ -482,6 +482,40 @@ panic_with_thread_kernel_state(const char *msg, arm_saved_state_t *ss)
 		    VALUE(state->far));
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * evergo (0154e): when the fault is a call through a corrupted pointer,
+	 * pc AND lr AND fp can all be zero (observed: instruction fetch abort at
+	 * 0, esr 0x86000005), and the register dump below then names nobody. The
+	 * interrupted stack still holds the caller frame records, so dump the
+	 * first words at sp right now, with printf - which RETURNS.
+	 *
+	 * Order matters: the register dump below goes through panic_plain, and
+	 * panic_plain is a macro for panic() (kern/debug.h), which never returns,
+	 * so anything after it is dead code the compiler strips. The dump must
+	 * run before it.
+	 *
+	 * printf output reaches the console and the SWEETLOG ring through
+	 * _cnputs, so the lines survive even when the screen has scrolled.
+	 * sleh.c's include chain never reaches kern/misc_protos.h, hence the
+	 * local declaration.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern int printf(const char *format, ...);
+
+		uint64_t ksp = state->sp;
+
+		if (ksp != 0) {
+			printf("SWEET STACK: sp 0x%016llx, dumping 48 words\n", ksp);
+			for (int i = 0; i < 48; i++) {
+				uint64_t word =
+				    *(const volatile uint64_t *)(ksp + (uint64_t)i * 8);
+
+				printf("SWEET STACK %02d @0x%016llx: 0x%016llx\n",
+				    i, ksp + (uint64_t)i * 8, word);
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	panic_plain("%s at pc 0x%016llx, lr 0x%016llx (saved state: %p%s)\n"
 	    "\t  x0:  0x%016llx x1:  0x%016llx  x2:  0x%016llx  x3:  0x%016llx\n"
 	    "\t  x4:  0x%016llx x5:  0x%016llx  x6:  0x%016llx  x7:  0x%016llx\n"
